-- Q02: INTEGRATION-2 explanation (883 -> 2286 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The scenario describes an agile project with the team that is trying to make projections related to the project completion, specifically, the schedule. Unlike the projects carried out using the waterfall/predictive project management approach, where project performance is measured against the performance measurement baseline, agile projects utilize various information radiators, such as the burndown and/or burnup charts, the cumulative flow diagram, etc. Traditional tools, such as the cost or schedule performance index, can also be used to aid with the analysis. These tools are examples of trend analysis. Trend analysis can be used to forecast future performance based on past results. By identifying the trend, the team may be able to understand how long it will take for the project to be completed, making trend analysis the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A SWOT analysis Incorrect. SWOT analysis is used to analyze the strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats of a project. In the scenario, the team is using burndown charts, CFD, and SPI, none of which is designed to analyze strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats, making SWOT analysis an incorrect answer. B Trend analysis Correct. Trend analysis can be used to forecast future performance based on past results. By analyzing the burndown chart, the cumulative flow diagram, and the schedule performance index of the project, the team members may be able to assess their future performance, including how long it will take to complete the project. C Schedule analysis Incorrect. Schedule analysis involves analyzing the project schedule. According to the scenario, the team is reviewing the burndown chart, the cumulative flow diagram, and the schedule performance index. While some of those tools can be used to analyze the project schedule, the overall task the team is involved in does not describe schedule analysis. D Variance analysis Incorrect. Variance analysis is a technique used to compare actual performance vs. planned performance as specified in the project baselines. Analyzing the burndown chart and SPI may provide some insights into the schedule variance. However, considering burndown charts, CFD, and SPI a variance analysis toolkit would be a stretch.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-2$int19$;

-- Q03: INTEGRATION-3 explanation (1000 -> 2417 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The scenario describes a project manager who is developing a draft of the project charter, implying the Develop Project Charter process is carried out. One of the typical activities performed while developing the charter is to review organizational process assets (OPAs), which may influence this process. One of the OPAs is the project governance framework which often provides information about the governance functions, processes, that are important when making decisions that are relevant to the project charter. One may argue that a stakeholder register can be created at the same time the project charter is developed and approved. While it's true, the question is asking to select the answer choice that represents an action performed 'during this process'. Since the scenario describes the Develop Project Charter process, any action that is associated with 'this process' would be a better option than an action associated with 'another process', even if that process can be done in parallel with the one described in the scenario. Therefore, as we always recommend selecting the best answer of those provided, studying the governance functions and processes to provide guidance and decision making is a better answer choice than identifying stakeholders and preparing the stakeholder register since the former is associated with the inputs to the Develop Project Charter process while the latter is not even part of this process. Details for Each Option: A Identify stakeholders and prepare the stakeholder register Incorrect. The question implies that you, as the project manager, are conducting the Develop Project Charter process. The stakeholder register is developed during the Identify Stakeholders process and not the Develop Project Charter process. B Study the governance functions and processes to provide guidance and decision making Correct. The project governance framework is part of the organizational process assets (OPA), which serve as an input into the Develop Project Charter process. C Update the business documents to correct any errors Incorrect. The project manager may make recommendations but does not update or modify the business documents since they are not project documents. D Review the benefits management plan Incorrect. The benefits management plan and business case comprise the business documents, and the question states that these documents have already been reviewed.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-3$int19$;

-- Q04: INTEGRATION-4 explanation (915 -> 2605 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$One of the project manager's responsibilities is providing recommendations and oversight to keep the project business case, project management plan, project charter, and project benefits management plan success measures in alignment with one another and with the goals and objectives of the organization. In this scenario, the company image for producing 'green' goods would be compromised if the non-sustainable wood was used in the product. Therefore, in order to uphold the company image, it is your job as a project manager to understand why the non-sustainable wood was prescribed for use in the business case. In the vast majority of our questions, consulting with the project sponsor is typically an incorrect answer. But not in this case. By contacting the sponsor and explaining your discovery to her, you can hopefully rectify the situation, making this the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Update the business case to specify sustainable wood to be used in the project Incorrect. The project manager does not have the authority to update the business case at will. The project sponsor is generally accountable for the development and maintenance of the project business case. B Continue to create the project charter according to the business case Incorrect. It is not ethical to deliberately ignore a problem that conflicts with company standards when you know it can be addressed. Ignoring the problem and proceeding blindly with the business case when you know something is not right, especially when there is another more "flexible" option of consulting with the project sponsor would be a better solution. What if there is simply a mistake in the business case? C Hold on the development of the project charter and ask the sponsor for advice Correct. Using non-sustainable wood in the product appears to be against the company's standards, and it is your job as a project manager to ensure the project aligns with the organization's goals and objectives. By contacting the sponsor, you can begin the process of understanding and possibly resolving the situation, making this course of action the best answer to the question asked. D Specify in the project charter the use of a sustainable wood source Incorrect. By specifying the wood source without consulting with the project sponsor, you are not confronting the issue with the aim of understanding the situation. Perhaps there is a way of using non-sustainable wood and at the same time meeting company standards. Regardless, just specifying the wood source is not the best action to take in this situation.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-4$int19$;

-- Q05: INTEGRATION-5 explanation (987 -> 2789 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$In order to obtain approval to proceed with project execution, the project management plan should be presented to the relevant stakeholders according to applicable policies and procedures. The applicable policies and procedures are typically part of the organizational process assets. These documents typically indicate, among other information, the policies and procedures relevant to the activities associated with the approval of the project management plan. By following these policies and procedures, the team member can determine who should be invited to a meeting aimed to review and approve the project management plan. Therefore, of the available choices, researching the organization process assets is the best response. Note, the scenario describes an agile project, yet managed using a project management plan. While this may sound contradicting, there is nothing in the scenario that limits the project to the use of a specific project management methodology with only tools, techniques, artifacts, assets, practices, etc., associated with that methodology. Therefore, it is reasonable to assume that the project as a whole is managed using hybrid methods. Details for Each Option: A Invite the customer only because it is an agile project Incorrect. Regardless of the nature of the project, agile or waterfall, the meeting attendees to review and approve the project management plan are typically specified in the policies and procedures included in the organizational process assets. Customers may or may not be specified among those who should be invited. B Find the answer in the organizational process assets Correct. A project management plan is the main output of the Develop Project Management Plan process. Obtaining approval of the plan from relevant stakeholders is crucial for the project manager to proceed with project execution. Following the policies and procedures included in the organizational process assets may help the team member determine who should be invited to the meeting. C Invite only the project sponsor and the project team Incorrect. While the project sponsor and the project team are important meeting attendees to review and approve the project management plan, other relevant project stakeholders should attend this meeting. Pay attention, choices that contain absolute statements such as ‘always’, ‘never’, ‘only’, etc., typically represent incorrect answers. D Consult the project schedule network diagram Incorrect. The project schedule network diagram is a project document that provides a graphical representation of the logical relationships among the project schedule activities. This document does not include information that would be helpful to determine who should be invited to the meeting to review the project management plan.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-5$int19$;

-- Q06: INTEGRATION-6 explanation (701 -> 1985 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Lean thinking uses the “Plan-Do-Check-Act” (PDCA) cycle. The PDCA cycle requires the team to do its work according to an explicit plan of execution, a 'model' if you will, that is guided by experience, good principles, and lessons that others have learned. This plan becomes the team’s standard process, and they do their work according to the plan, then stop and check their experience against the plan. Based on their observations, they decide how to adjust the plan, what to change, and what to keep doing. Then they begin to plan again. This approach helps project teams with their efforts of continuous improvement and tailoring their processes to achieve the best possible outcomes. Details for Each Option: A The team members are not expected to perform their work according to an explicit plan of execution. Incorrect. The PDCA cycle requires that the team performs its work according to an explicit plan of execution that is guided by experience, good principles, and lessons learned. B The team members are only expected to use the PDCA cycle for their development efforts. Incorrect. The PDCA cycle is not limited to only the development cycle but becomes part of the mindset in all aspects of the team’s work. C The team members are expected to perform their work according to an explicit plan of execution. Correct. The PDCA cycle requires that the team performs its work according to an explicit plan of execution that is guided by experience, good principles, and lessons learned. The PDCA cycle is the basis for continuous improvement efforts. D The team members are only expected to use the PDCA cycle once during a development cycle. Incorrect. The PDCA cycle is not limited to only one iteration during the development cycle. Team members do their work according to the plan, then stop and check their experience against the plan. Based on their observations, they decide how to adjust the plan, what to change, and what to keep doing. Then they begin to plan again.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-6$int19$;

-- Q07: INTEGRATION-7 explanation (568 -> 1884 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question states that project planning is about to begin which suggests that the processes of the Initiating Process Group have been completed. The Initiating Process Group consists of two project management processes, Develop Project Charter and Identify Stakeholders. Therefore, the project charter and a list of stakeholders are available prior to project planning. At a high level, the project charter ensures a common understanding by the stakeholders of the key deliverables, milestones, and the roles and responsibilities of everyone involved in the project. Details for Each Option: A Share the project charter with the stakeholders Correct. At a high level, the project charter ensures a common understanding by the stakeholders of the key deliverables, milestones, and the roles and responsibilities of everyone involved in the project. B Email a copy of the project scope statement to the stakeholders Incorrect. The project scope statement is an output of the Define Scope process which is part of the Planning Process Group. The question states that project planning has yet to begin; therefore, the project scope statement is not available to share with stakeholders. C Refer the stakeholders to the project management plan Incorrect. The project management plan is created during the Planning Process Group. However, based on the scenario described, project planning has not yet started. Therefore, the project management plan does not exist. D Cannot be done yet as stakeholders have not been identified at this point in the project Incorrect. Stakeholders are identified during the initiation stage of the project which is completed just before project planning. The question states that project planning is about to begin which suggests that the processes of the Initiating Process Group have been completed. Therefore, a list of stakeholders should be available.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-7$int19$;

-- Q08: INTEGRATION-8 explanation (1000 -> 2525 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$All answers choices would provide you with information, techniques or knowledge that may help you improve your chances of project success. However, as always, we are looking for the best answer of the choices available. Updates to the lessons learned register is one of the outputs from the Close Project or Phase process and is an input to many project management processes. The lessons learned register is a project document that contains the knowledge gained throughout the previous project phase and learnings that you can apply for the second phase of your project. Lessons learned register contains the information about what worked well in the previous phase of the project and what can be improved and should be reviewed by any project manager when starting a new phase to improve the chances of project success. Therefore, of the available choices, reviewing the lessons learned register is the best answer to the question asked. Note, one may argue that a lessons learned repository would be a better answer choice. Indeed, the lessons learned repository could have been a plausible answer. However, this choice is not provided. Therefore, we have to select the best option of those available, which, in this case, is the lessons learned register. Details for Each Option: A Meet with the outgoing project manager Incorrect. Although meeting with the outgoing manager is a good idea to get information relevant to the project and the past phase, it will only provide you feedback and perspective from one person. B Take a project management training class Incorrect. Taking a project management training class may help you to learn the fundamentals and practices of project management; however, such class will not provide you with the specific information about the previous phase of your project. C Review the lessons learned register Correct. The lessons learned register is a project document that contains information about risks, tools, techniques, and learnings about past phases of the project. Reviewing the lessons learned register would help you avoid making the same mistakes and learn from what worked well. D Check the phase closure documents Incorrect. The project or phase closure documents consist of formal documentation that indicates the completion of the project or phase. Although it may be beneficial for the new project manager to review project phase closure documents to see what deliverables were completed, it would not provide a comprehensive look at what worked well in the last phase.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-8$int19$;

-- Q09: INTEGRATION-9 explanation (812 -> 2100 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Using existing knowledge and creating new knowledge to achieve the project's objectives and contribute to organizational learning is the definition of the Manage Project Knowledge process. The question is asking to find an enterprise environmental factor (EEF) which contributes (in other words, serves as an input) to this process. Organizational, stakeholder, and customer culture is an example of the EEFs that are among the inputs to the Manage Project Knowledge process. The culture of trusting working relationships and a no-blame culture is particularly important in managing knowledge on a project as it allows people to feel comfortable sharing information. The incorrect answer choices, while being inputs to the process, can be eliminated simply because none of them is considered an EEF. Details for Each Option: A Lessons learned register Incorrect. The lessons learned register provides information on effective practices in knowledge management and serves as an input to the Manage Project Knowledge process implied by the scenario. However, the lessons learned register is a project document rather than an enterprise environmental factor. At the same time, due to the iterative nature of project management, the lessons learned register is an output from the process, while the question is looking for a contributing factor, e.g., an input. B Knowledge management plan Incorrect. The knowledge management plan is a made-up term. Even if this plan did exist, it would have been considered a component of the project management plan rather than an EEF. C Trusting working relationships Correct. Having trusting working relationships and a no-blame culture allows the team, the stakeholders, and the customer feel comfortable sharing information, thus contributing to the creation of new knowledge and achieving the project's objectives. D Interpersonal and team skills Incorrect. While having strong interpersonal and team skills would likely contribute to the Manage Project Knowledge process inferred by the scenario, these skills are not considered enterprise environmental factors.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-9$int19$;

-- Q11: 510893 explanation (885 -> 2554 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Data and metrics are very important to track, monitor, and control any project. This is true regardless of which project management approach is used. When teams and organizations shift from one approach to another or use various approaches for different projects with a program, it is common for multiple tools and software to be used. The intent of these tools is to assist the teams, stakeholders, and project organizations in managing their data and methodology. However, too many or divergent tools may cause more administration and maintenance. Therefore, project and program governance must determine which data is most relevant and worth the teams' time. The balance between collecting and communicating project data with the resource load to enter and upkeep the data can be difficult, but needed decision. Details for Each Option: A Direct the teams to continue entering the data in the various systems Incorrect. Data entry and tracking are important to the project, program, and organizational governance. However, the resource load demand to enter and upkeep the data may or may not be equal to the gain. Entering data just for data’s sake is not an efficient use of resources. Evaluation of the appropriate data and tools to be used should be done first. B Determine what tools and data provide the appropriate oversight Correct. As teams utilize various project management approaches, it is not uncommon that multiple tools, dashboards, and repositories of data are used. Project/program governance needs the data, but the teams cannot be ruled by the tool, especially if the resource effort to enter and maintain the data does not equate to meaningful insight and governance. C Switch all projects in the program to the agile project management approach Incorrect. This course of action may (or may not) resolve the issue of duplicate systems. However, it is reasonable to assume that the approach for each project in the program has been selected for a reason. Therefore, switching all projects to one approach may harm the intended benefits. There is not enough information in the scenario to determine if agile is applicable to or needed for all projects. D Pause all data entry until a valuable solution can be determined Incorrect. Collecting, tracking, and controlling project data is important. Project teams, stakeholders, project and program managers rely on the data to manage the project/program and make informed decisions. Pausing all data entry until a valuable solution can be determined is unlikely to be the best approach.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$510893$int19$;

-- Q12: INTEGRATION-12 explanation (623 -> 1928 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question states that the project manager is about to start the development of the project charter, which implies that the Develop Project Charter process is just getting started. Interviews, which is a data gathering technique, may be used during the Develop Project Charter process to gather information on high-level requirements, assumptions or constraints, approval criteria, and other information from stakeholders by talking to them directly. Therefore, of the available choices, conducting interviews with the project sponsor and other relevant stakeholders is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Review the assumption and constraints log Incorrect. The assumption log is sometimes referred to as the assumptions and constraints log. The assumption log is created as an output of the Develop Project Charter process, and the question states that the project manager has yet to begin this process. Therefore, the assumption log is not available to serve as a source of information at this point in the process. B Conduct interviews with the project sponsor and other relevant stakeholders Correct. Interviews is a data gathering technique, which may be used during the Develop Project Charter process to gather information on high-level requirements, assumptions or constraints, approval criteria, and other information from stakeholders by talking to them directly. C Analyze the risk register Incorrect. The risk register is created as an output of the Identify Risks process, which occurs during project planning, whereas the question suggests that the project is still in the initiation stage with the development of the project charter. D Perform the Qualitative Risk Analysis process since the assumption log is an output of this process Incorrect. The assumption log is an output of the Develop Project Charter process rather than the Perform the Qualitative Risk Analysis process.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-12$int19$;

-- Q13: INTEGRATION-13 explanation (641 -> 2252 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question describes a high-risk project in the initiation stage where project risk is a significant concern. Although the identification of individual project risks is not formally performed until project planning, the project charter should contain a summary of the overall project risk. The project manager should consider consulting individuals or groups who have a specialized knowledge or training in risk identification. Seeking assistance from a PMI Risk Management Professional (PMI-RMP) ® is an example of expert judgment. Under the circumstances described in the scenario, it is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Double the size of the management and contingency reserves Incorrect. According to the scenario, the project is in initiation. Management and contingency reserves are determined during project planning and should be adequate to address the identified risks (contingency reserves) and unknown-unknowns (management reserves). Not only is it premature to allocate reserves during project initiation, but also doubling the size of the reserves (which are still unknown at this point) without proper planning is not an acceptable project management practice. B Ensure the risk identification process is extremely rigorous Incorrect. Identifying risks is the key to effective risk management, with the level of rigor and detail tailored to the requirements of the project. However, the Identify Risks process is part of project planning, while the scenario suggests the project is still in the initiation, making this choice an incorrect answer. C Tell the sponsor that you have managed similar projects Incorrect. While it may be comforting for the sponsor to know that you have managed similar projects, merely stating your experience does not address the essence of the question. A choice other than this one is more effective in taking into account the low risk tolerance of the project sponsor when the project charter is being developed. D Consult with a PMI Risk Management Professional (PMI-RMP)® Correct. This answer choice describes expert judgment. The project manager should consider consulting with individuals or groups who have specialized knowledge or training in risk identification.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-13$int19$;

-- Q14: 510673 explanation (1620 -> 3252 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Project teams should constantly review the process they use to deliver value for the project. If inefficiencies or waste are found, the team should seek options to adjust the process to improve efficiency and eliminate waste. However, time spent tracking conformance to process is the time lost from delivering value for the project. Therefore, project teams should dedicate just enough time conforming to the process without unnecessarily detracting from the time needed to produce project deliverables. The same goes for the processes and policies the organization puts in place for the project teams to conform to. The new policy requires all employees to report their work in the time tracking system. However, the policy is not specific enough about the level of details needed to be reported. Should the reporting only include the project name? Or should the team members report on specific deliverables they developed, tasks they performed, meetings they attended, calls they made, lunch breaks they took? The management encourages reporting as many details as possible. 'Encourages' is the keyword here. It's not a requirement, not a directive, not a demand. It's just encouragement. Therefore, while some may want to appease the management and report as many details as possible, the recommendation the project manager should make to the team under these circumstances is to spend just enough time to conform to the new company policy so that the negative effect on the value delivered by the team will be minimized. Details for Each Option: A Proceed as before without reporting the work for the sake of delivering value for the project Incorrect. The policy should be complied with even if it takes away the team's time that is better spent delivering value for the project. It's the ambiguity around the details to be reported is that should be addressed before the team starts wasting its time recording unnecessary information. Proceeding without reporting the work is not the best choice. B Record as many details as possible to meet the management's request Incorrect. Management holds power; hence, employees may feel the need to keep the management happy. However, recording as many details as possible may negatively impact the project's progress. Since the policy is not specific enough, and management encourages, not demands, recording apparently unnecessary details would not be recommended. C In each sprint, create a user story to cover the time needed to record the work Incorrect. Recording as many details as possible, as encouraged by the management, is not the best use of the time that is better spent creating value for the project. Creating user stories to cover the time needed to record the work is an even further waste of time. Teams should spend just enough time to conform to policies without violating them. D Spend just enough time to conform to the new company policy Correct. Whether we like them or not, policies are in place to be complied with. In the scenario, the policy seems not clear enough about the details of work to be recorded. Therefore, to be in compliance on the one hand and to continue delivering value, on the other hand, the team should spend just enough time to conform to the new policy.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$510673$int19$;

-- Q15: INTEGRATION-15 explanation (467 -> 1641 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The key elements of a benefits management plan include target benefits, strategic alignment, a timeframe for realizing benefits, benefits owner, metrics, assumptions, and risks. Identification of a set of options to be considered for addressing the business problem or opportunity is a key element of the project business case and should not be included in the benefits management plan. This element is most likely what was marked for removal by the PMO. Details for Each Option: A Expected tangible and intangible value to be gained by the implementation of the project Incorrect. The expected tangible and intangible value to be gained by the implementation of the project is an example of the target benefits. The target benefits are a key element of the benefits management plan. Therefore, this is not the element that was marked for removal. B A set of options to be considered for addressing the business opportunity Correct. Identification of a set of options to be considered for addressing the business problem or opportunity is a key element of the project business case and should not be included in the benefits management plan. This element is most likely what was identified by the PMO. C A timeframe for realizing the benefits of the project Incorrect. A timeframe for realizing the benefits of the project is one of the key elements of the benefits management plan. Therefore, this answer choice can be eliminated. D Measures to be used to show benefits realized Incorrect. Metrics, the measures to be used to show the benefits realized, is a key element of the benefits management plan, making this answer choice incorrect.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-15$int19$;

-- Q16: INTEGRATION-16 explanation (1000 -> 3110 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$A development approach refers to the means used to create and evolve the product, service, or result during the project life cycle. The selection of the development approach depends on multiple variables, such as the product, project, and organization. A successful transition from a predictive development approach to an adaptive one requires a change of some or all of those variables. Organizational structure, culture, and organizational capability are among the parameters that will need to be adjusted to support the transition. An organizational structure with many levels, a rigid reporting structure, and excessive bureaucracy works better for projects carried out using a predictive approach. To be agile, the organization would need to flatten its structure and rely on self-organizing teams empowered to make decisions concerning their work. In the scenario, the agile coach finds an organization that has not adapted its structure to the stated goal of transition to agile. Should the organization have had the right mindset going top-down from the executive level throughout the organization, the transition would have had a chance to succeed. Without that mindset, the transition is doomed. Therefore, of the choices provided, the lack of the mindset was the most likely reason for the failure. The incorrect answer choices describe proper agile practices, which, if implemented properly, would have helped rather than prevented the successful adoption of agile. Details for Each Option: A The mindset of the executive level has not shifted. Correct. Just stating that "from now on the organization will be agile" is not enough. The transition entails shifting the mindset top-down, starting at the executive level. The organizational structure issues that the agile coach found indicate the mindset has not shifted, making this choice the most likely reason the transition failed. B Too much empowerment has been given to the teams. Incorrect. Adaptive (agile) methods promote empowered teams that can make decisions concerning their work. In the scenario, the organizational structure has many levels and a rigid reporting structure, implying the teams were not empowered enough (or at all) to practice agile successfully. 'Too much' empowerment would have unlikely led to failure. C Stakeholders were involved too early in the projects. Incorrect. Project teams have to respond quickly to the change inherent to adaptive environments. Early stakeholder feedback is essential to ensure the teams build a product that meets the stakeholders' expectations. Therefore, having stakeholders involved early in the project would have helped, not hindered, the transition to agile. D Frequent product demos distracted the teams from creating value. Incorrect. Frequent product demonstrations (reviews) to gain stakeholder feedback are typical of agile practices. While the time the team spends demonstrating the product can indeed be spent creating value, doing so without stakeholder feedback may result in a product no one needs. Hence frequent demos were unlikely the reason for transition failure.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-16$int19$;

-- Q18: INTEGRATION-18 explanation (1000 -> 2289 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Clearly defined document ownership ensures accountability, updating schedules reduces delays, and real-time synchronization protocols address inconsistencies across time zones. This approach ensures that the repository functions effectively for collaboration and supports global teams while maintaining version control and accessibility. A global project involves multiple cross-functional teams in different time zones. The artifacts (important project documents, deliverables, and records) are stored in a centralized cloud-based repository for accessibility. Despite regular version control and accessibility checks, stakeholders report inconsistent updates, causing decision-making delays. Stakeholder access and collaboration issues—Even though the repository is centralized, updates do not effectively reach stakeholders. Version integrity problems—Document changes are not consistently reflected, leading to outdated or conflicting information. Global team coordination – Different time zones and team structures mean updates may not always be communicated effectively. The best approach to resolve document inconsistencies in a global project is structured artifact management (Answer C). Clearly defining ownership, schedules, and real-time syncing ensures accountability, consistency, and collaboration. While partially helpful, other options fail to address the root problem or introduce new risks. Details for Each Option: A Implement automated notifications for every document update to inform all stakeholders in real-time. Incorrect. Automated notifications may overwhelm stakeholders and don’t solve inconsistencies in updates or accountability. B Introduce a decentralized repository system for each region to reduce dependency on a single repository. Incorrect. A decentralized repository risks misalignment and complicates version control. C Establish clearly defined document ownership, update schedules, and real-time synchronization protocols. Correct. Defined ownership, schedules, and synchronization ensure effective artifact management in global projects. D Transition to physical artifacts for high-priority documents to ensure reliability and accuracy. Incorrect. Transitioning to physical artifacts is impractical for global projects and hinders collaboration.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-18$int19$;

-- Q19: INTEGRATION-19 explanation (508 -> 1781 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The scenario describes a large project which will be conducted in four phases. Multiphase projects, like the one described by the question, will typically include a kick-off meeting at the beginning of each phase. Consider that each phase of a multiphase project is treated much like an individual project in that most or all of the processes are repeated for each phase. Therefore, the best response to the project sponsor's question is to conduct a kick-off meeting at the start of each phase. Details for Each Option: A During execution of the first phase Incorrect. The project kick-off meeting may be conducted during project execution for a large project. However, this answer choice is incomplete as a multiphase project will typically have a kick-off meeting at the beginning of each phase. Therefore, this is not the best answer of the available choices. B As part of the Planning Process Group of the first phase Incorrect. A project kick-off meeting might be conducted during project planning, particularly for a small project. However, the scenario indicates that this is a large multiphase project, and a multiphase project will typically have kick-off meetings at the start of each phase. C At the start of each phase Correct. Multiphase projects, like the one described by the question, will typically include a kick-off meeting at the beginning of each phase, making this choice the best answer to the question asked. D As part of pre-project work Incorrect. During pre-project work, the business case and the benefits management plan are developed. The project isn't even authorized until the project charter is drafted and approved as part of project initiation. Therefore, during pre-project work is not an appropriate time to conduct a project kick-off meeting.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-19$int19$;

-- Q20: INTEGRATION-20 explanation (357 -> 1841 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question is asking where to find information on lessons learned from past projects. The lessons learned repository is an organizational process asset which serves as a store of historical information about lessons learned from past projects. Therefore, researching the organizational process assets is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Review the lessons learned register Incorrect. The lessons learned register is a source for information on the ‘current’ project, while the question states that you want to know if there is any relevant information from ‘past’ projects. Information on past projects is contained in the lessons learned repository rather than the lessons learned register. B Research the organizational process assets Correct. The lessons learned repository is an organizational process asset which serves as a store of historical information about lessons learned from past projects. Therefore, researching the organizational process assets is the best answer to the question asked. C Meet with a project manager who managed similar past projects Incorrect. While the project manager of similar past projects may have some relevant information, this information reflects only one opinion. Checking the organizational process assets is a better answer choice because the lessons learned repository is more likely to have complete and accurate information reflecting the views of all project team members. Therefore, this answer choice can be eliminated. D Check the enterprise environmental factors for lessons learned from past projects Incorrect. The lessons learned repository is an organizational process asset and not an enterprise environmental factor. The enterprise environmental factors are not a source of information on past projects, making this choice an incorrect response.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-20$int19$;

-- Q21: INTEGRATION-21 explanation (493 -> 2169 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The needs of the project determine which components of the project management plan are needed. Some projects may not require all of the possible components of the project management plan, and their inclusion would be superfluous. In these cases, the project manager should determine the needs of the project and build a project management plan with the appropriate level of detail. Creating an overly elaborate project management plan is not an effective use of project resources. Details for Each Option: A Ignore the sponsor's request since the project management plan is the responsibility of the project manager Incorrect. Ignoring the sponsor's request is unlikely to be the best course of action for the project manager. The project manager should explain to the sponsor that the contents of the project management plan may vary from project to project and are determined according to the needs of the project. B Explain that the needs of the project determine which components of the project management plan are included Correct. The needs of the project determine which components of the project management plan are needed. The project manager should determine the needs of the project and build a project management plan with the appropriate level of detail. C Update the plan as requested by the project sponsor since the plan's components are determined by the sponsor Incorrect. The project sponsor may offer valuable feedback on the content of the project management plan which should be taken into account. However, the needs of the project determine which components of the project management plan are included. Creating an overly elaborate project management plan is not an effective use of project resources. The project manager should explain to the sponsor why certain components have been excluded from the plan. D Ask the meeting participants to vote for those components of the project management plan they think should be included Incorrect. While voting is an appropriate technique to be used in meetings, the decision on what components should be included in the project management plan is made based on the project needs rather than on voting.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-21$int19$;

-- Q22: INTEGRATION-22 explanation (731 -> 1691 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$An adaptive project management life cycle allows the project stakeholders to review and adapt to changing conditions continuously. 'Just in time' is an approach that is rooted in manufacturing, where materials or components are delivered only immediately before they are needed to minimize inventory costs. In the case of the adaptive project management life cycle, the teams plan and commit to plans only at the point of time that they need to. If the team plans too far ahead of time, conditions are likely to change, necessitating a change of plan. To avoid the potential waste of time speculating on solutions, planning in an adaptive project management life cycle model is typically limited to the next iteration. Details for Each Option: A Only used for low risk projects Incorrect. An adaptive project management approach is not limited to low-risk projects only. B Limits scope change requests to a minimum Incorrect. An adaptive project management approach does not limit the scope change requests to a minimum. Rather, it is designed to manage many scope change requests since change is an assumption that has already been built into the approach and process. C Just-in-time planning Correct. To avoid potential waste of time speculating on solutions, planning is confined to the next iteration. D Up-front planning for the entire project Incorrect. An adaptive project management approach operates in situations where uncertainty abounds, so the planning is typically limited to the next iteration. Up-front planning for the entire project is most suitable where scope, schedule, budget and other project constraints are relatively well known up-front during the project planning.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-22$int19$;

-- Q23: 520286 explanation (1057 -> 2533 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Both approaches, iteration-based agile and flow-based agile, comprises of cycles that deliver completed product increment(s) (feature(s)). Each cycle includes the process of determining requirements, analysis, design, development, and testing. The main difference is that in iteration-based agile, each iteration is of the same length (timebox), and each timebox results in a completed feature(s). In flow-based agile, the time it takes to complete a feature is different for each feature. The team pulls features from the backlog based on the team's capacity to start work. The team defines its workflow with columns on a task board (e.g., a Kanban board) and manages the work in progress for each column. According to the scenario, the customer wants the features delivered as the features are completed even if the time between the deliverables will vary from one feature to another. Therefore, of the choices available, flow-based agile is the best fit for the customer needs and, therefore, the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Iteration-based agile Incorrect. In iteration-based agile, the team works, as the name implies, in iterations to deliver completed features. In the scenario, however, the customer prefers to have the features delivered as they are completed, regardless of the time that passes between the deliverables. Therefore, imposing timeboxes would prevent some of the features from being delivered right after they are completed, since the team will have to wait for the end of the iteration. On the other hand, big features will need to be sliced and delivered over the course several iterations, thus adding complexity to the process. B Flow-based agile Correct. In flow-based agile, the team pulls features from the backlog based on the team's capacity to start work rather than on an iteration-based schedule. Since it's more important for the customer to have the features delivered as they are ready rather than based on the timeboxed schedule, flow-based agile would be the best approach. C Predictive-based agile Incorrect. While some hybrid combinations may exist, "predictive-based agile" is a made-up term. D Any type of agile Incorrect. Since the scenario describes a project with unclear requirements that will frequently change as the project progresses, theoretically, any type of agile life cycle would work for this project. However, we are always looking for the best answer of those available. Another answer choice more accurately addresses the question asked.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$520286$int19$;

-- Q24: INTEGRATION-24 explanation (1000 -> 2618 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Mandatory regulations usually require documentation to prove the product or service is compliant with rules and regulations. Launching the site without the documentation will expose the project, the client, and the delivering organization to risk. The expression "the customer is always right" does not apply in this case. The documentation is a part of the project. Without completing it, the project is not considered done. Many industries, such as finance, healthcare, government, have compliance-related projects and requirements. Agile teams may dislike completing documentation and may consider it as a non-value added activity that goes against one of the Agile Manifesto values. However, the values should not be abused. In this scenario, notwithstanding agile, according to the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) regulations, the documentation is a mandatory requirement and, therefore, should be completed in spite of the customer's desire and sponsor's insistence to launch the site immediately. Hence, carrying out one more sprint to complete the required documentation is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Launch the site to ensure that the customer and sponsor are satisfied Incorrect. Ensuring customer satisfaction is an essential aspect of every project. However, launching the site just to satisfy the customer and sponsor and taking the chance of not being compliant with regulations may put the whole project at risk and eventually negate the positive outcome of being earlier to the market. B Raise the paperwork as a roadblock at the next standup meeting Incorrect. The documentation is not a roadblock; it is part of the project since it's required by the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard regulations. C Carry out one more sprint to complete the required documentation Correct. Documentation is required for certain projects with regulations and/or mandatory compliance. Documentation user story was added to the project likely to protect the company from fines and potential contract disputes. Therefore, the team has to carry out one more sprint to complete the documentation. D Remove the documentation user story from the sprint backlog Incorrect. The product owner is the only one who decides on what user stories to remove from the sprint. In this case, the documentation user story not only adds value to the project but is also required by the regulations. Therefore, regardless of who decides on the product backlog, removing the documentation user story from the sprint backlog would add risk to the project and customer.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-24$int19$;

-- Q25: INTEGRATION-25 explanation (715 -> 1845 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The purpose of the project kick-off meeting is to communicate the objectives of the project, gain the commitment of the project team for the project, and explain the roles and responsibilities of each stakeholder. Each of these points is captured by the incorrect answer choices. However, the question is asking to select a response that is the 'least' likely to describe the purpose of the project kick-off meeting. The identification of the key project deliverables and success criteria happens during a meeting to develop the project charter rather than a project kick-off meeting, making the identification of the key project deliverables and success criteria the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Communicate the project objectives to the stakeholders Incorrect. Communicating the objectives of the project is one of the project kick-off meeting’s objectives., making this choice an incorrect answer. B Identify the key project deliverables and success criteria Correct. Identification of the key project deliverables and success criteria are among the items on the agenda of meetings dedicated to the development of the project charter. Since the question is asking to select the answer choice which is least likely to be associated with the kick-off meeting, this choice is the best answer. C Gain the commitment of the stakeholders for the project Incorrect. Gaining the commitment of the team and the stakeholders for the project is one of the purposes of the project kick-off meeting and is, therefore, an incorrect answer. D Explain the roles and responsibilities of key stakeholders Incorrect. An explanation of the roles and responsibilities of each stakeholder should be included in the project kick-off meeting agenda. Therefore, for this negatively worded question, this choice is an incorrect answer.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-25$int19$;

-- Q26: INTEGRATION-26 explanation (390 -> 1688 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Discussing user stories with the appropriate stakeholders, especially with the customer, is an essential part of a sprint planning meeting. This discussion is necessary for the team to confirm their understanding of the user stories. Of the available choices, only discussing the user stories with the appropriate stakeholders is the most appropriate action for the agile team. Details for Each Option: A Breaking down the user stories into epics Incorrect. Breaking down the user stories into tasks, not epics, is part of the sprint planning meeting process. Such breakdown is required for the team members to start the actual coding of the user story. An epic could be decomposed into user stories, but not the other way around. B Demonstrating the product increment to the product owner Incorrect. Demonstrating the product increment to the product owner occurs during a sprint review meeting and not a sprint planning meeting. Additionally, this action would not be beneficial in elaborating user stories. C Ensuring that all user stories are fully elaborated before including them in the product backlog Incorrect. Ensuring that all user stories are fully elaborated before including them in the product backlog runs contrary to the agile best practices. In the product backlog, the user stories are allowed to be coarse-grained. However, when including the user stories into the sprint backlog during the sprint planning meeting, the user stories should be fully elaborated. D Discussing each user story with the appropriate stakeholders Correct. Discussing user stories with the appropriate stakeholders is necessary for the team to confirm their understanding of the user stories.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-26$int19$;

-- Q27: INTEGRATION-27 explanation (1000 -> 2623 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Incorporating an incremental product development approach would permit the project team to deliver product increments as they are developed. The requesting organization (client) can begin receiving benefits as soon as the first product increment is completed, accepted, and deployed. Thus, the requesting organization would not have to wait the full 18 months of the project term to begin receiving value. Creating a minimum viable product (MVP) is common practice with software development projects, as is the case in the scenario described. The MVP can be developed and released while the project is still in execution, and the benefits associated with the MVP can begin being realized. Additional features can then be developed and released as they are completed until a fully functional product with all required features is delivered. Note, the question implies that the project will be managed using a traditional waterfall framework. However, by blending an incremental lifecycle with the waterfall model, the framework changes to hybrid. Incorporating an incremental lifecycle will require changes to the proposed project management plan. For example, the development approach portion of the project management plan will have to be updated along with other components. Details for Each Option: A Apply fast-tracking to tasks that reside on the critical path Incorrect. Fast-tracking might shorten the time to delivery. However, fast-tracking is a schedule compression technique, and the question stipulates that all schedule compression techniques have already been used to shorten the schedule. B Incorporate incremental product development within a predictive model Correct. Incorporating an incremental product development approach would allow the requesting organization (client) to begin receiving benefits as soon as the first product increment is completed. C Crash the schedule by hiring additional software developers Incorrect. Crashing is a schedule compression technique used to shorten the schedule duration by adding resources. However, the scenario states that the schedule has already been compressed as much as possible. Therefore, any opportunities to crash the schedule have already been exploited. D Submit a change request to update the project management plan Incorrect. According to the scenario, the project is in planning. Change requests are not required during project planning. Even if the project was in execution, it is unclear as to what exactly should have been changed in the project management plan. One option, however, is modifying the development approach specified in the plan.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-27$int19$;

-- Q28: INTEGRATION-28 explanation (913 -> 2350 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$It is possible for a project to be successful from a scope/schedule/budget viewpoint, and to be unsuccessful from a business perspective. This situation can occur when there is a change in the business needs or the market environment before the project is complete. A change in the competitive landscape may very well cause a project to meet its objectives but fail to deliver the intended benefits. For example, let's say that a pharmaceutical company develops a drug to treat the symptoms associated with a certain disease. Just as the new drug is ready for market, a competing company discovers a cure for the same disease. The drug developed to treat the disease has just been made obsolete by the cure. The project to develop the drug may have been successful in meeting its objectives for scope, schedule, and budget, but would fail to deliver the intended business value from selling the drug. Details for Each Option: A The competitive landscape changed Correct. A project can meet all of its stated objectives and still be unsuccessful from a business perspective. This situation can occur when there is a change in the business needs or the market environment before the project is complete. B It is not possible for a project to meet its objectives and not be successful from a business perspective Incorrect. It is possible for a project to be successful from a scope/schedule/budget viewpoint, and to be unsuccessful from a business perspective. This situation can occur when there is a change in the business needs or the market environment before the project is complete. C The project manager failed to execute the project management plan properly Incorrect. There is no information presented by the question to indicate that the project manager failed to execute the project management plan properly. On the contrary, the fact that the project was successful in meeting objectives for scope, schedule, and budget suggests that the project management plan was successfully implemented. D The benefits analysis was fundamentally flawed Incorrect. Although it is possible, there is no information presented by the question to suggest that the benefits analysis was flawed. While this answer choice may be correct, there is another response which provides a more plausible explanation for the project to fail from a business perspective.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-28$int19$;

-- Q29: INTEGRATION-29 explanation (700 -> 1845 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Finalizing activities for the project in which all work required to produce deliverables has been complete implies the project enters its final stage, closure. As part of the Close Project or Phase process, the final product, service, or result is handed over to a different group or organization that will operate, maintain, and support it. The final product, service, or result transition is an output of the Close Project or Phase process. This output refers to the transition from one team/group/department/organization to another. Transferring the ownership of deliverables to the assigned stakeholders in accordance with the project plan is performed to facilitate project closure. Details for Each Option: A Release the project team Incorrect. The question implies the project has entered its last stage - closure. Work completed does not necessarily mean all activities are completed. There are many activities associated with project closure that may require the project team. Releasing the team is a premature action. B Close contracts with vendors Incorrect. Contracts are closed as part of the Control Procurements process. During project closure implied by the scenario, the project manager and team confirm the formal acceptance of the vendor's work. Regardless, there is not enough information in the question to determine if there are any contracts on the project. C Hand over the final product Correct. As part of the Close Project or Phase process implied by the scenario, the final product, service, or result is handed over to a different group or organization that will operate, maintain, and support it. D Update the project charter Incorrect. The project charter is a document that formally authorizes the project and the project manager. It is rare when a project charter is updated, especially at the end of the project.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-29$int19$;

-- Q30: INTEGRATION-30 explanation (666 -> 2025 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The project manager is looking for a way to share her and others' experience, insights, opinions, and "know-how" or her tacit knowledge within the organization. Tacit knowledge is personal, requires context, and is difficult to express. This differs from explicit knowledge that can be easily recorded using words, pictures, and numbers. This question asks, which is the least effective choice for capturing tacit knowledge. Using information management systems such as a lessons learned register may be a good way to capture explicit, codified knowledge gained after each project, but is the least effective method to capture and share tacit knowledge. Details for Each Option: A Hosting workshops, seminars, and information sessions Incorrect. This answer choice is listed as one of many techniques that are used by projects to promote and share tacit knowledge within an organization or project team. B Providing work shadowing and reverse shadowing opportunities Incorrect. Providing work shadowing and reverse shadowing opportunities are listed as effective methods of tacit knowledge capture and transfer within a project. Since the correct answer will be the choice that is least likely to promote and improve tacit knowledge sharing, this is an incorrect choice. C Promote attending knowledge-sharing events such as conferences and other networking events Incorrect. Attending knowledge-sharing events such as conferences and networking events are listed in the PMBOK guide as examples of how to promote tacit knowledge sharing. Networking, including informal social interaction and online social networking, can be useful for starting knowledge-sharing conversations with specialists. D Create a lessons learned register for every project Correct. Of the answer choices, this is the least effective tool for capturing and sharing tacit knowledge. A lessons learned register is a great tool for capturing explicit, codified knowledge gained after each project, but that is not the primary objective in this situation.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-30$int19$;

-- Q31: INTEGRATION-31 explanation (1000 -> 2906 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The development approach is one of the components of the project management plan. The development approach describes the development approach taken to produce the product, the service, or the result of the project. Examples of development approaches include predictive, iterative, agile, or hybrid. In the scenario described, some of the deliverables are developed using Scrum (which is an example of agile) while others are produced using traditional methods. This implies the project, as a whole, is executed using a hybrid project management approach. Therefore, the project management plan should reflect this information as the development approach selected for the project. For example, during the development of the project management plan, it is possible that the project team determined that there were going to be many changes to some of the project deliverables due to their nature (e.g., a software application) and, therefore, a Scrum framework would be best to develop those deliverables. Going with this example, requirements for other deliverables, on the other hand, were determined upfront favoring the traditional project management approach. Therefore, of the choices provided, showing the development approach within the project management plan to the project sponsor would be the best course of action for the project manager to take to address the sponsor's request. Details for Each Option: A Show the project life cycle description to the project sponsor Incorrect. The project life cycle description provides the series of phases that the project passes through from its initiation to its closure. The project life cycle description does not contain information about the development approach used to produce the project deliverables. B Tell the sponsor to talk to the project team so that they can explain how the product is developed Incorrect. Although the project team members are likely to know about the approach they are using to develop the product, sending the sponsor to the team will possibly interrupt the team's work. It's the project manager’s responsibility to communicate with the sponsor and other stakeholders and let the team do their work with as few interruptions as possible. C Suggest reviewing the project charter together with the sponsor Incorrect. The project charter is the document that authorizes the existence of a project, names the project manager and provides them with the authority to apply organizational resources to project activities. The project charter is unlikely to have the information about the development approach to be used on the project. D Show to the sponsor the development approach specified in the project management plan Correct. The project management plan specifies the development approach (such as predictive, iterative, agile, hybrid) that will be used to produce the product, the service, or the result of the project.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-31$int19$;

-- Q32: 511689 explanation (559 -> 2086 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Using existing knowledge and creating new knowledge for a project is essentially the definition of the Manage Project Knowledge process. When the knowledge that the project has is compared to its knowledge needs, then the gaps in knowledge can be identified and addressed. Specifically, the resource breakdown structure, as it pertains to the Manage Project Knowledge process, includes information on the composition of the team and may help to understand what knowledge is available as a group and what knowledge may be missing. Details for Each Option: A Bid documents Incorrect. Bid documents are used to solicit information, quotations, or proposal from prospective sellers. First, it is not clear from the scenario if the project involves sellers. Second, even if it does, the information included in the bid documents is limited to the work outsourced to the sellers. B Resource breakdown structure Correct. The resource breakdown structure is a hierarchical representation of resources by category and type. The resource breakdown structure is one of the project documents that serve as an input for the Manage Project Knowledge process described in the scenario. The resource breakdown structure includes information on the composition of the team and may help to understand what knowledge is available as a group and what knowledge is missing C Quality metrics Incorrect. A quality metric is a description of a project or product attribute and the way the Control Quality process will verify compliance to it. Quality metrics have little to do with using and preservice knowledge on a project. D To-complete performance index Incorrect. The to-complete performance index (TCPI) is a measure of the cost performance that is required to be achieved with the remaining resources in order to meet a specified management goal. The TCPI is just one piece of information, while the goal of the project manager in the scenario provided is to use existing knowledge and create new knowledge for the project. It does not seem reasonable that the TCPI can help in accomplishing this goal.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$511689$int19$;

-- Q33: INTEGRATION-33 explanation (566 -> 1591 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Building shelter in a region that has been recently destroyed by a natural disaster is an example of a project initiated to address a social need. Other examples include but are not limited to providing potable water systems, latrines, and sanitation education to communities suffering from high rates of infectious diseases. Note, the scenario describes a construction company, not a governmental organization. The fact of being a nongovernmental entity is important for the project to meet the definition of an initiative that addresses a social need. Details for Each Option: A Market demand Incorrect. Building a very long-lasting smartphone battery to satisfy the needs of the users is an example of a project to meet market demand. In the scenario, the region has suffered from a natural disaster, leaving the population without shelter. A shelter is considered a basic human being need rather than a market demand. B New technology Incorrect. A company authorizes a project to build a new rocket which will be able to travel at speeds close to the speed of light based on the advances in fuel and materials technologies. Building a shelter for population suffered from a natural disaster is typically not based on new technologies. C Social need Correct. Of the choices provided, building a shelter in a region that has been destroyed by a natural disaster is best described by addressing a social need. D Legal requirement Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario suggests that building a shelter in a region that has been destroyed by a natural disaster would satisfy a legal requirement.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-33$int19$;

-- Q34: 545601 explanation (551 -> 1712 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Projects are selected for many reasons. Not all desired projects can be undertaken by an organization. Decisions as to which projects an organization is to take on are often part of an executive steering committee's responsibility. To get a certain project sponsored and chartered, the Director in this scenario must present the merits and value to the business to the steering committee. Upon selection and approval, the project charter for the project can be commissioned and the project made official. Details for Each Option: A Advocate for the project with the highest NPV Incorrect. The scenario does not provide project selection methods used by the steering committee. Additionally, according to the scenario, the Director strongly believes that a system upgrade is a project to be selected. Therefore, advocating a project with the highest NPV (unless it's the system upgrade which is not clear from the question) makes no sense. B Select a project sponsor Incorrect. Based on the scenario, it seems that the Director or the executive steering committee would likely be the project sponsor. Even if not, it is unclear if the Director has the authority to select the project sponsor. C Present the merits of the system upgrade project Correct. The Director is attempting to gain support for the system upgrade project. Making a case for the project to the steering committee is the best action to take to achieve this goal. D Select a project manager Incorrect. The project has not yet even been selected. Therefore, selecting a project manager is a premature action. Additionally, there is not enough information in the question to determine if the Director has the authority to select project managers.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$545601$int19$;

-- Q35: INTEGRATION-35 explanation (375 -> 1645 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Continuous feature delivery with incremental releases is beneficial in terms of ROI. For example, rather than waiting 12 or 18 months for new software features, incremental delivery can deliver features to customers quarterly or even more frequently, and allow customers to take advantage of first-to-market opportunities that would be lost by waiting 18 months. Details for Each Option: A Agile project management methods improve product time-to-market for the customer. Correct. Continuous feature delivery through the use of incremental releases is beneficial regarding ROI. Incremental delivery can deliver features to customers more frequently, and allow customers to take advantage of first-to-market opportunities. B Agile project management methods eliminate all time and effort spent on documentation. Incorrect. Although project documentation should diminish when using agile project management practices, some documentation may still be required. C Agile project management methods develop a comprehensive set of features. Incorrect. The Standish Group Study estimates that over 40% of software features were never used, and only 20% were used often or always. Using agile methods may prevent the development of costly but unused features, which can be instrumental in improving ROI. D Agile project management methods involve multitasking between several different projects to maximize utilization of available resources. Incorrect. Multitasking results in task switching penalties, which are a form of waste. Agile methods are based on the principles of eliminating waste wherever possible, which reduces cost and helps improve ROI.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-35$int19$;

-- Q36: INTEGRATION-36 explanation (678 -> 2808 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Project leaders need to recognize, evaluate, and respond to system interactions. When agile teams need to coordinate their activities to address interdependencies between various projects, a scaling approach is often used. One of the more popular scaling approaches is the Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe ® ). A key principle of SAFe ® is the application of systems thinking. Systems thinking entails taking a holistic view of how projects and project parts interact with each other. Systems thinking will be critical in dealing with complex projects with interdependencies so the organization can maximize the delivery of business value through the various value streams. Watch to see how Cornelius Fichtner explains the correct answer during a live webinar: Please note that Cornelius focuses primarily on explaining the correct answer and not necessarily why the others are incorrect. Also note that on the technical side, the video will continue playing beyond the explanation, so you will have to click the video to stop it. To find more videos like this, please click here to visit our YouTube channel Details for Each Option: A Hiring a program manager to ensure decision making across the projects is centralized Incorrect. A program manager is responsible for coordinating activities between projects, and this role should be helpful in addressing interdependencies. However, the question indicates that the Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe®) is being employed. With SAFe®, decision-making is decentralized rather than centralized. B Applying systems thinking by taking a holistic view of how the projects and their parts interact Correct. One of the key principles of the Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe®) is the application of systems thinking. Systems thinking entails taking a holistic view of how projects and project parts interact with each other. Systems thinking will be critical in addressing the interdependencies that exist between the projects and project teams. C Ensuring that each project operates within a silo led by a dedicated scrum master Incorrect. Under the circumstances described in the scenario, the organization needs to better coordinate activities between the various project teams. Operating each project within a silo would serve as a significant impediment to coordinating activities and addressing the interdependencies between the projects. D Having the scrum masters remove distractions by limiting communication between project teams Incorrect. Removing impediments is one of the scrum master's responsibilities as a servant leader. However, when interdependencies exist between projects, efforts need to be taken to coordinate the activities between the project teams. With limited communications and isolated from one another, the teams won't be able to work effectively.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-36$int19$;

-- Q38: INTEGRATION-38 explanation (948 -> 2552 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The language of the scenario implies the project has both predictive and adaptive attributes. For example, project execution is carried out using adaptive practices (iterations) while the encompassing project management approach is predictive (as evident from the terms, such as project initiation, planning, execution, the approval of the project management plan, etc.). In this hybrid environment, project processes should be tailored to the needs of the project and its stakeholders, who should be continuously engaged and provide their ongoing feedback. In adaptive/hybrid projects, competing priorities, changing dynamics, and technological advances may cause the project constraints and success criteria to change significantly, as described in the scenario. Therefore, to ensure the project is moving within constraints and toward goals that reflect the latest information, continuous stakeholder engagement feedback is crucial. Details for Each Option: A Colocating the project sponsor with the team and the project manager Incorrect. Colocation involves placing project team members in the same physical location to enhance their ability to perform as a team. Colocating the project sponsor with the team and the project manager makes little sense and is unlikely to prevent the situation described in the scenario. B Continuously engaging with stakeholders and getting their ongoing feedback Correct. On adaptive projects (as the one described in the scenario), shifting priorities and changing dynamics may cause project constraints and goals to become obsolete. Continuous engagement with stakeholders who provide ongoing feedback will help ensure the project is moving within constraints and toward goals that reflect the latest information. C Terminating the project before obtaining the authorization to start it Incorrect. Terminating the project before obtaining the authorization to start it would have definitely prevented the situation from occurring since no project means no issues. While such an explanation may sound sarcastic, this answer choice was intentionally designed to be implausible. D Abandoning agile practices in favor of a predictive project management approach Incorrect. Projects with shifting priorities and technological advances are best suited for agile practices. In the scenario, the product is developed through iterative processes, implying the use of agile practices. Abandoning agile and switching to the less flexible predictive methodology would likely exacerbate the situation rather than resolve it.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-38$int19$;

-- Q39: INTEGRATION-39 explanation (698 -> 1589 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Identifying key deliverables based on the business requirements in order to manage customer expectations and direct the achievement of project goals is one of the tasks that should be performed during project initiation (pre-project work). The identified key deliverables, such as the new product, service, or result that the project is intended to satisfy, are then included as an element of the project charter. Of the available options, only key deliverables is an element of the project charter. Therefore, of the choices provided, it is reasonable to assume that identification of the key deliverables is what the project manager might have overlooked during the pre-project work. Details for Each Option: A Identification of detailed requirements Incorrect. During project initiation, only high-level requirements are identified. Detailed requirements are determined as part of the Collect Requirements process during project planning. B Identification of subjective project objectives Incorrect. Measurable project objectives is an element of the project charter. The project objectives must be measurable, not subjective, in order to determine if the project successfully achieves its objectives. C Identification of key deliverables Correct. The identification of key deliverables is performed during pre-project work and is an element of the project charter. D Development of a project scope statement Incorrect. The project scope statement is created during the Define Scope process. The Define Scope process is performed during project planning and not during pre-project work.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-39$int19$;

-- Q40: INTEGRATION-40 explanation (1000 -> 2906 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$In the scenario described, you have reviewed the business case, benefits management plan, a memorandum of understanding (MOU), as well as determined project objectives, success criteria, and high-level requirements. These activities are performed during the Develop Project Charter process. Inputs to the Develop Project Charter process are the business case, agreements (MOU is an agreement), enterprise environmental factors (EEF), and organizational process assets. EEF refer to conditions, not under the control of the project team, that influence, constrain, or direct the project. Labor rules and regulations in the target country can constrain the project and are not under the control of the project team. Therefore, these rules and regulations are considered EEF. Understanding these components of the EEF is likely to assist you the most in completing the Develop Project Charter process. Therefore, of the choices provided, understanding rules and regulations in the target country is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Understanding labor rules and regulations in the target country Correct. The scenario suggests that you are performing the Develop Project Charter process and enterprise environmental factors (EEF) are an input to that process. EEF refer to conditions, not under the control of the project team, that influence, constrain, or direct the project. Labor rules and regulations in the target country can constrain the project and are not under the control of the project team. Of the available choices, understanding these rules and regulations is likely to assist you the most in completing the Develop Project Charter process. B Creating a detailed description of the project deliverables Incorrect. A detailed description of the project deliverables is created during project planning, as part of defining the project scope. As implied by the scenario, you are conducting the Develop Project Charter process which is part of project initiating, not project planning. C Creating a document that describes how requirements will be analyzed, documented, and managed Incorrect. The requirements management plan describes how requirements will be analyzed, documented, and managed. The requirements management plan is created during the Plan Scope Management process, not during the Develop Project Charter process, which is described in the scenario. D Creating a document that describes how and when the benefits of the project will be delivered and how the benefits will be measured Incorrect. The benefits management plan describes how and when the benefits of the project will be delivered and how they will be measured. The question is asking what 'else' will assist you in completing the process described in the scenario. Since, according to the scenario, you have already reviewed the benefits management plan, this choice can be eliminated.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-40$int19$;

-- Q41: INTEGRATION-41 explanation (617 -> 2256 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Agreements, or contracts, are an output from the Conduct Procurements process and an input to the Close Project or Phase process which is, according to the scenario described, you are going to perform. A termination clause is one of the major components of an agreement. Therefore, before closing the project, you must first review the contractual rights and obligations with regard to contract termination and fulfill these obligations. In other words, to ensure that activities required by the contract are performed when closing the project, you must understand the termination clause in the contract. Details for Each Option: A Release payments to your supplier Incorrect. While it is reasonable to assume that you should release payments to your supplier when the project is closed, either as planned or terminated prematurely, the first thing you should do when a project is terminated is to check the termination clause of the contract. In the contract, you can determine who is authorized to receive payments, what portion of the work should be completed for the payments to be released, etc. B Relieve the project team from their duties Incorrect. Although as part of the Close Project or Phase process you need to relieve the project team from their duties, the first thing you should do when a project is terminated is to check the termination clause of the contract. The project team members may be required to perform some of the project closure activities. Hence, relieving the team from their duties may prevent the project from being closed properly and is, therefore, unlikely what you should do next. C Identify lessons learned Incorrect. Identification of lessons learned is done as part of the Close Project or Phase process. However, this activity is not as urgent as checking the termination clause of the contract which will determine the next steps to be taken when a project is terminated. D Check the termination clause of the contract Correct. When a project is closed, either as planned or terminated prematurely, the Close Project or Phase process should be performed. However, in this scenario, before closing the project, you must first understand the termination clause in the contract to decide on what should be done next.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-41$int19$;

-- Q42: INTEGRATION-42 explanation (850 -> 2265 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Predictive project life cycles are characterized by an emphasis on the specification of requirements and detailed planning during the beginning phases of a project. Agile life cycles, on the other hand, are characterized by the progressive elaboration of requirements based on short iterative planning end executing cycles. Projects that are managed using hybrid methods combine elements of both approaches. Depending on how well or poorly the requirements are defined at the beginning of the project, the elaboration of requirements on hybrid projects will tend to either the predictive or agile side of the project life cycle continuum. In any case, requirements on hybrid projects will be elaborated at periodic intervals during delivery, with the time between the intervals being dependent on the level of detail of the requirements. Details for Each Option: A Defined during the transition to the customer Incorrect. Defining requirements during the transition to the customer make no sense. Requirements should be either defined up-front as it's done with traditionally managed projects or, at least, drafted at the beginning of the project and then progressively elaborated (with iterative projects) or at the beginning of each iteration (with agile projects). B Elaborated at periodic intervals during delivery Correct. When a hybrid life cycle is selected for a project, requirements can be roughly defined up-front and then elaborated at periodic intervals during delivery. Depending on the way requirements are specified, the project hybrid life cycle may tend to be either more predictive or agile. C Defined up-front before development begins Incorrect. Requirements are defined up-front before development begins in predictive, not hybrid, project life cycles. Such up-front planning is possible when requirements are stable and are unlikely to change significantly as the project progresses. D Elaborated as often as needed during delivery Incorrect. Requirements are elaborated as often as needed during delivery in agile, not hybrid, project life cycles. Typically requirements on agile projects are vague, and only high-level knowledge about the product exists. Requirements are elaborated as often as needed as more information becomes available.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-42$int19$;

-- Q43: INTEGRATION-43 explanation (427 -> 2013 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question indicates that the Close Project or Phase process is underway. During project closure, the organizational process assets are updated to include finalized project documents, operational and support documents, project or phase closure documents, and the lessons learned repository. Therefore, of the available choices, updating the organizational process assets is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: A Close procurements with the vendors Incorrect. Procurements are closed as part of the Control Procurements process, not the Close Project or Phase process which is, as implied by the question, the process being carried out in the scenario. B Formally sign off the project deliverables Incorrect. Deliverables that meet the acceptance criteria are formally signed off and approved by the customer or sponsor as part of the Validate Scope process rather than the Close Project or Phase process. In the Close Project or Phase process, the project manager 'confirms' the delivery and formal acceptance of the deliverables. While the difference is subtle, formal acceptance and the confirmation of the acceptance are two different things. One can think of the confirmation of acceptance as a checkbox that should be ticked during project closure: "Have the deliverables been formally accepted during the Validate Scope process?" If Yes, check the box. If No, there is a problem. C Update the enterprise environmental factors Incorrect. The question implies the Close Project or Phase process is being carried out in the scenario. Updates to the enterprise environmental factors are not among the outputs of this process. D Update the organizational process assets Correct. Organizational process assets (OPAs) updates are among the outputs of the Close Project or Phase process described in the scenario. The updates to the OPAs may include finalized project documents, operational and support documents, project or phase closure documents, and the lessons learned repository.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-43$int19$;

-- Q44: INTEGRATION-44 explanation (402 -> 1958 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$As a project is transferring the ownership and operation of the project's product to another team or the customer, transferring knowledge is important. This transference is not exclusively collecting the knowledge and handing it over. It requires training, educating, and communicating the contents, organization, and purpose of the knowledge and associated wisdom to the receiving entity. Details for Each Option: A Communicating project status to the sponsor; issuing the final report; notifying the project team of release readiness Incorrect. Communication is important, but it is not the sole means of knowledge transfer, especially when that communication is only internal and not to those who are to own that knowledge moving forward. The three activities listed in this answer choice are internal to the project organization. B Training end users in the receiving organization; finalizing documentation; conducting final readiness assessments Correct. Knowledge transfer requires some form of training and documentation. This list is not complete but is the most comprehensive of those provided, making it the best answer to the question asked. C Backing up software files; uploading project documents to PMO repository; verifying all asset management records Incorrect. Collecting and making knowledge accessible is an important first step to knowledge sharing, but it is not enough on its own. Knowledge transfer requires information on how to access and use the collected knowledge. D Communicating the project status to the end users; releasing the project team; verifying product completion Incorrect. The status of the project is typically communicated to the project stakeholders rather than the end users. Verifying the product completion is not transferring the knowledge, but validating the scope and that the work was done. Releasing the project team before knowledge is transferred to the receiving organization may be a premature action.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-44$int19$;

-- Q45: INTEGRATION-45 explanation (607 -> 2231 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Based on the scenario, it can be inferred that the project is in initiating. When initiating new projects in any organization, examining and assessing the successes and failures of past projects can serve as excellent information to support the project(s) under consideration. Learning from failed projects can provide valuable information towards the success of another project. Repeating the same mistakes should not occur if the organization reviews past projects, assesses the lessons learned, and applies them to the next project, as in the form of the project chartering in this scenario. Details for Each Option: A Include the assumption in the project charter that this project may also fail to set stakeholder expectations accordingly Incorrect. All projects may fail. There is no need to call out that failure as an option and a possibility. Stakeholder expectations can be managed in other ways. B Make certain that the team members from the failed project are not involved in the new project to avoid repeating the failure Incorrect. There is not enough information in the question to determine if the team was the reason for the failure of the previous project and should be excluded from working on the new one. Furthermore, including team members from previous, failed projects may actually be a benefit, not a liability, for the next project. They bring experiences and lessons learned that could be shared and applied. C Review the lessons learned from the failed project to incorporate this knowledge into the project charter of the new project Correct. Positioning a project for success takes in information and assessments from many angles. This includes the lessons learned from previous projects - successful or failed. D Gather information from the project manager and the team of the failed project and use this information to plan the new project Incorrect. While meeting with the project manager and the team of the previous failed project may provide insights into the reasons for the failure of that project, the information they will share may be faded. Additionally, it is unclear if those individuals are still available. The information should be readily available in the lessons learned repository.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-45$int19$;

-- Q47: INTEGRATION-47 explanation (1000 -> 2010 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$Agile Manifesto value #2 emphasizes working software over comprehensive documentation. Agile teams focus on adapting and evolving the working software, but at the same time, they also have to anticipate or spend time in planning, architecture, design, and requirements definition. Agile planning focuses on early delivery of working software. In this scenario, the team should start building working software but should not do so in a haphazard fashion. Wireframes are skeletal frameworks of websites. They are often used to layout content and functionality, so the project has a visual structure and basis for development. As wireframes are just a schematic and not typically overly elaborate, it is an example of early and adequate planning. They are not over planned and do not involve comprehensive documentation. Reactive thinking, refactoring, and delaying run contrary to Agile Manifesto value #2 as it is also important to get going on a project with what is known about it and in general it is better to prevent issues and errors sooner rather than later. Details for Each Option: A Focus on reactive thinking to improve the speed of development Incorrect. Reactive thinking will slow down rather than improve the speed of development. By anticipating and being pro-active, one is better able to mitigate risks, prevent rework, etc B Depend on refactoring at later stages to fix errors made early on Incorrect. The sooner one corrects errors and the root cause of an issue (e.g. poor programming practice), then one might be able to avoid having to refactor at later stages. C Arrange prioritized elements in the form of a wireframe Correct. Wireframes assist the project's development by laying out content and functionality so the project has a visual structure and basis for development. D Delay requirements gathering until more is known about the system Incorrect. Some upfront planning and requirements gathering is required for the features and user stories to be developed in early iterations.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-47$int19$;

-- Q48: INTEGRATION-48 explanation (539 -> 2276 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The sequence 'create the project charter, create stakeholder register, inform stakeholders of the approved project charter' is the only option with the correct action sequence before project planning starts. It is important that the project manager informs the stakeholders of the project charter once it is approved and before moving into planning processes in order to ensure a common understanding of the key deliverables, milestones, and the roles and responsibilities of all those involved in and impacted by the project. Details for Each Option: A Perform stakeholder analysis, create stakeholder register, resolve issues raised by stakeholders Incorrect. Although the sequence is correct, stakeholder issues are typically resolved as part of project execution in the Manage Stakeholder Engagement process. Since the question requires a sequence of activities before project planning starts, this choice is an incorrect answer. B Define scope, collect requirements, create the work breakdown structure Incorrect. The Collect Requirements process precedes the Define Scope process, making this sequence incorrect. Additionally, this answer choice represents actions associated with project planning rather than actions to be taken prior to planning, as asked by the question. C Plan stakeholder engagement, create power/interest grid, manage stakeholder engagement Incorrect. The creation of the power/interest grid is done as part of the Identify Stakeholders process and is not created after the Plan Stakeholder Engagement process is carried out. Additionally, Manage Stakeholder Engagement is an executing process, while the question is asking to select a sequence that is completed before project planning begins. Therefore, this answer choice can be eliminated. D Create the project charter, create stakeholder register, inform stakeholders of the approved project charter Correct. The first project management process is Develop Project Charter, followed by the Identify Stakeholders process. Once the project charter is authorized, the stakeholders should be made aware of the approved project charter before project planning begins to ensure all those impacted by the project understand the project deliverables, milestones, and their roles and responsibilities.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-48$int19$;

-- Q50: INTEGRATION-50 explanation (904 -> 2227 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $int19$The question states that the project charter is being developed, which occurs during project initiation. Brainstorming is a data gathering technique which may be useful in the development of the project charter. Brainstorming is a specialized meeting led by a facilitator which can be used to gather data and solutions or ideas from stakeholders, subject matter experts, and team members when developing the project charter. Note, the question is asking for the activity which is 'least' likely to be conducted during the development of the project charter. The communications management plan is not created until later in the project during the Plan Communications Management process. Therefore, the project manager would not be able to follow the communications management plan in determining the meeting attendees, making this the 'least' likely activity to be conducted in this scenario. Details for Each Option: A Inviting relevant stakeholders to attend the meeting Incorrect. A stakeholder is an individual, group, or organization that may affect, be affected by, or perceive itself to be affected by a decision, activity, or outcome of a project. Since stakeholders have a vested interest in the project, it may be beneficial to seek their feedback to incorporate into the project charter. B Ensuring that a software engineer joins the meeting Incorrect. A software engineer can be considered a subject matter expert. Since the project in this scenario involves software development, a software engineer will be able to provide technical insights that may influence the development of the project charter. C Arranging for a facilitator to lead the meeting Incorrect. A brainstorming session is conducted in a group environment. To ensure the meeting is run effectively and kept on track, arranging for a facilitator to lead the meeting is likely to be one of the actions taken by the project manager. D Following the communications management plan to determine meeting attendees Correct. The question states that the project charter is being developed which occurs during project initiation. The communications management plan is not created until later in the project during the Plan Communications Management process.$int19$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $int19$INTEGRATION-50$int19$;
